WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.141 --> 00:00:09.282
A circle of rocks in a field in southern England, near the city of Salisbury, has intrigued many

2
00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.083
different people for thousands of years.

3
00:00:11.763 --> 00:00:15.044
It is called Stonehenge, and it is the world's most famous megalith.

4
00:00:15.844 --> 00:00:20.826
Some of the stones used to make this megalith are over six meters tall; its massive size and

5
00:00:20.946 --> 00:00:24.147
old age have contributed to its fame throughout the world.

6
00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:30.305
But it is the mystery of its construction, and the many legends that surround it, that make Stonehenge

7
00:00:30.365 --> 00:00:32.346
the earth's most visited stone monument.

8
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:39.171
Even after years of historical research and scientific investigation, there is still no clear understanding of how or

9
00:00:39.271 --> 00:00:40.532
why Stonehenge was built.

10
00:00:41.172 --> 00:00:45.555
Engineers ponder, "How exactly were these huge rocks transported and arranged?"

11
00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:51.279
Archaeologists ask, "What did this circle of rocks mean for ancient civilizations?"

12
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.523
Investigators of the supernatural wonder, "Was this the site of a pagan temple, or a marker for alien

13
00:00:57.563 --> 00:01:05.034
visitors?" Many people have developed theories about Stonehenge─its origin and its purpose─but so far none of these theories

14
00:01:05.094 --> 00:01:06.215
have been proven correct.

15
00:01:06.795 --> 00:01:11.720
Stonehenge is made up of several concentric circles of stones that are surrounded by a circular ditch.

16
00:01:12.821 --> 00:01:18.326
The sheer size of the stones and their imposing arrangement make Stonehenge far more sophisticated than any other

17
00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:20.668
building or monument built in Britain at the time.

18
00:01:21.468 --> 00:01:26.260
All of the larger stones once had horizontal stones balanced on top of them, but many of these

19
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:32.163
stones have fallen down or been damaged over the centuries─some have even been removed to build nearby homes

20
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:32.803
and bridges.

21
00:01:34.244 --> 00:01:39.026
The construction of Stonehenge began around 3100 BC and took place in three stages.

22
00:01:39.526 --> 00:01:42.647
The indigenous people of England were the first to work on Stonehenge.

23
00:01:43.268 --> 00:01:46.969
These were ancient farmers who had a deep reverence for circles and symmetry.

24
00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:53.372
They dug a circular ditch about one hundred meters in diameter and built an embankment just inside it.

25
00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:58.689
They made two entrances in the ditch: a large one, with an avenue leading away from it, and

26
00:01:58.729 --> 00:01:59.290
a small one.

27
00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.671
They then dug 56 evenly spaced holes within the giant circle.

28
00:02:04.211 --> 00:02:09.132
These holes probably held wooden posts at one time, but those disappeared long ago.

29
00:02:09.852 --> 00:02:13.753
Work on Stonehenge started again in about 2500 BC.

30
00:02:14.174 --> 00:02:19.035
This time between sixty and eighty stones were arranged in two circles in the middle of the circle.

31
00:02:19.575 --> 00:02:25.149
The relatively small stones that were used are called bluestones because they look blue when they get wet.

32
00:02:25.729 --> 00:02:31.493
The four-ton bluestones originally came from mountains in Wales, which are 380 kilometers away.

33
00:02:32.053 --> 00:02:37.976
Transporting these stones all this way was an astonishing feat, considering their size and the fact that most

34
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:39.898
of the surrounding area was forested.

35
00:02:40.598 --> 00:02:45.941
The stones were probably dragged down to the sea, floated around the coast and up rivers on huge

36
00:02:46.001 --> 00:02:48.022
barges, and then dragged overland.

37
00:02:49.123 --> 00:02:53.581
After the bluestones were put in place, work stopped on Stonehenge for a few hundred years.

38
00:02:54.341 --> 00:02:59.862
Then, in 2300 BC, the third and final stage of Stonehenge's construction began.

39
00:03:00.463 --> 00:03:06.224
Thirty enormous sandstone rocks were arranged in a large circle, within the ring of post holes but around

40
00:03:06.244 --> 00:03:07.785
the outer ring of bluestones.

41
00:03:08.485 --> 00:03:12.226
Each weighed around 28 tons and measured up to 9 meters high.

42
00:03:12.806 --> 00:03:16.767
It is estimated that it would take six hundred men to move just one of these stones!

43
00:03:17.387 --> 00:03:21.981
Holes were dug two meters deep for the stones to sit in, and the stones were probably pulled

44
00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:25.742
into an upright position with the help of ropes and wooden frames.

45
00:03:26.322 --> 00:03:31.443
Then, ten more sandstone blocks were set at the center of the site in five pairs, with large

46
00:03:31.523 --> 00:03:32.924
rocks balanced on top of them.

47
00:03:33.524 --> 00:03:35.965
These stones were arranged in the shape of a horseshoe.

48
00:03:36.485 --> 00:03:41.926
Finally, a pointed stone called the Heel Stone was placed at the beginning of the avenue extending from

49
00:03:41.966 --> 00:03:42.767
the large entrance.

50
00:03:43.687 --> 00:03:47.168
When it was completed, the site was a magnificent thing to behold.

51
00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.923
People have had a lot of time to wonder about Stonehenge, since it was built thousands of years

52
00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:56.546
ago. Its size and unique construction have inspired many legends about its origin.

53
00:03:57.147 --> 00:04:02.411
According to one legend, Stonehenge was created when a group of giants dancing in a circle suddenly turned

54
00:04:02.451 --> 00:04:02.931
to stone.

55
00:04:03.592 --> 00:04:09.756
Many people who know this legend do say that Stonehenge's massive stones somewhat resemble the shape of giants

56
00:04:09.796 --> 00:04:10.537
holding hands.

57
00:04:11.277 --> 00:04:14.960
Others believe that Stonehenge owes its existence to the magician Merlin.

58
00:04:15.656 --> 00:04:21.341
A bloody battle was fought between the British and the Saxons on Salisbury Plain, the location of Stonehenge,

59
00:04:21.721 --> 00:04:23.062
in 450 AD.

60
00:04:23.743 --> 00:04:28.227
After the battle the British king wanted a monument for his soldiers that had been slain in the

61
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:35.232
battle. So, according to the legend, his magician, Merlin, transported a stone circle, which was called the Giant's

62
00:04:35.292 --> 00:04:37.054
Ring, from Ireland to Britain.

63
00:04:37.775 --> 00:04:40.237
There are even more theories about Stonehenge's purpose.

64
00:04:40.937 --> 00:04:44.060
Some people believe that it is a signpost for visiting UFOs.

65
00:04:44.628 --> 00:04:47.789
Stonehenge might have another relationship with outer space as well.

66
00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:52.390
Some people believe that the stones correspond to the positions of the planets and moons.

67
00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.591
As a result they think that Stonehenge was used to predict eclipses.

68
00:04:57.231 --> 00:05:03.173
Most archaeologists agree that the stones were deliberately arranged to coincide with points in the sun's annual path.

69
00:05:03.833 --> 00:05:09.095
On the summer solstice, the sun's first rays shine directly into the center of the monument between the

70
00:05:09.135 --> 00:05:13.916
stones that form the central horseshoe, as the sun rises directly over the Heel Stone.

71
00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.683
Most experts believe that this phenomenon is too perfectly calculated to be an accident.

72
00:05:20.183 --> 00:05:24.246
This leads many to believe that Stonehenge was built as a site for the celebration of summer and

73
00:05:24.286 --> 00:05:28.528
winter solstices, which were very important times of the year for ancient cultures.

74
00:05:29.369 --> 00:05:34.772
Another theory suggests that ancient circles like Stonehenge may have originally been enclosures for cattle.

75
00:05:35.412 --> 00:05:41.056
Remains of fences and gates found at Coupland Henge, which is eight hundred years older than Stonehenge, support

76
00:05:41.076 --> 00:05:41.616
this idea.

77
00:05:42.188 --> 00:05:47.490
The theory states that cattle were moved into the enclosure during certain seasons; then, as time wore on,

78
00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:52.431
structures like Coupland Henge and Stonehenge began to be used for purely religious purposes.

79
00:05:53.112 --> 00:05:59.113
But until archaeologists can ascertain the true origin of Stonehenge, legends about it will continue.

80
00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:06.256
One difficulty archaeologists face is that the ancient people who lived on or visited Stonehenge left very little

81
00:06:06.296 --> 00:06:11.727
garbage. Studying trash is one of the best ways archaeologists can use to try to understand the life

82
00:06:11.967 --> 00:06:13.628
and habits of an ancient society.

83
00:06:14.149 --> 00:06:20.173
The lack of garbage at Stonehenge has led some archaeologists to conclude that Stonehenge was sacred ground.

84
00:06:20.773 --> 00:06:25.356
As one scientist put it, Stonehenge was a "clearly special place where you didn't litter."

85
00:06:26.077 --> 00:06:31.941
Stonehenge is the most intact and elaborate monument of its kind, but the remains of over a thousand

86
00:06:31.981 --> 00:06:35.123
stone circles exist throughout Britain and northern France.

87
00:06:35.763 --> 00:06:41.108
Some of them are small, like Keel Cross in County Cork, Ireland, which is only three meters in

88
00:06:41.148 --> 00:06:47.553
diameter. The largest stone circle is in Avebury, England; it covers over 28 acres and encircles a whole

89
00:06:47.613 --> 00:06:53.438
village. So, though Stonehenge is the center of many legends and mysteries, it is far from unique.

90
00:06:54.059 --> 00:06:58.742
Today Stonehenge is a UNESCO World Heritage Site that attracts 750,000 tourists each year.

91
00:07:00.956 --> 00:07:06.858
Recently people have been complaining about the cars, buses, and trucks that thunder past it on nearby roads.

92
00:07:07.438 --> 00:07:13.420
These annoyances have resulted in plans to remove the roads and restore the megalith's original grassland setting.

93
00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:20.062
In this peaceful environment, Stonehenge and the mystery of its origin and purpose will surely awe visitors for

94
00:07:20.122 --> 00:07:21.082
many years to come.
